WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:04.080
Whether they're meant to give us clues about
season 3 or to simply give us all a reason

00:00:04.080 --> 00:00:08.120
to rewatch once we're done with our first
binge, here are some of the small details

00:00:08.120 --> 00:00:11.160
you probably missed in The Umbrella Academy's
second season.

00:00:11.160 --> 00:00:14.140
Spoiler warning for season two of The Umbrella
Academy.

00:00:14.640 --> 00:00:19.720
At the end of season 2, the time-traveling
team successfully returns to 2019, only to

00:00:19.730 --> 00:00:23.770
learn the Umbrella Academy seems to have been
replaced with the Sparrow Academy, apparently

00:00:23.770 --> 00:00:25.400
led by a not-so-dead Ben.

00:00:25.580 --> 00:00:28.740
"I-I'm just happy that we're home and together
again."

00:00:29.080 --> 00:00:29.860
"Home?

00:00:29.860 --> 00:00:31.520
This isn't your home."

00:00:31.820 --> 00:00:35.140
These sparrows aren't the only birds flying
around the second season, though.

00:00:35.140 --> 00:00:39.380
While you may not notice them before the Sparrow
Academy reveal, the official Umbrella Academy

00:00:39.391 --> 00:00:45.079
Instagram page posted that there are exactly
43 sparrows in season 2 a significant number

00:00:45.079 --> 00:00:51.280
since there were said to be exactly 43 superpowered
children born on October 1, 1989 in the show's

00:00:51.280 --> 00:00:51.920
mythology.

00:00:51.920 --> 00:00:56.440
Perhaps the easiest example to remember is
Harlan's sparrow toy, which we see him levitating

00:00:56.440 --> 00:00:59.620
in the back seat of his mother's car towards
the end of the finale.

00:00:59.620 --> 00:01:03.739
In "A Light Supper," eagle-eyed viewers can
spot a sparrow logo on the piece of paper

00:01:03.739 --> 00:01:08.220
the Handler gives Five with the location of
the Commission's executive board a logo that

00:01:08.220 --> 00:01:11.540
mysteriously disappears when Five looks at
it in the following episode.

00:01:11.540 --> 00:01:16.060
Other examples of sparrow logos show up on
the dairy truck the Swedish assassins steal,

00:01:16.060 --> 00:01:19.970
on the front door of the liquor store that
Klaus visits, on the room service menu Lila

00:01:19.970 --> 00:01:24.280
looks at, and on yet another on a piece of
paper in Old Man Five's briefcase.

00:01:24.280 --> 00:01:28.420
We haven't discovered them all yet, but the
prospect of finding them isn't a bad motivation

00:01:28.420 --> 00:01:29.940
to rewatch season 2.

00:01:30.340 --> 00:01:34.700
One of the bigger surprises in the season
2 finale is the crazy reveal that Lila is

00:01:34.700 --> 00:01:39.300
one of the superpowered children born in 1989,
just like the seven members of the Umbrella

00:01:39.300 --> 00:01:39.880
Academy.

00:01:39.880 --> 00:01:43.940
Similar to Mimic of Marvel Comics' X-Men,
Lila can use other people's powers to her

00:01:43.940 --> 00:01:44.800
own advantage.

00:01:44.800 --> 00:01:47.600
And we see her gift on full display at the
Coopers' farm.

00:01:47.600 --> 00:01:52.020
While at first it looks like the Academy has
slam-dunked the Commission in the season finale,

00:01:52.020 --> 00:01:54.600
Lila is able to use their powers to even the
playing field.

00:01:54.840 --> 00:01:55.980
"How are you doing this?"

00:01:56.380 --> 00:01:58.660
"Anything you can do, I can do better."

00:01:58.980 --> 00:02:02.680
But what you may not realize is this isn't
the first time in the series that she's used

00:02:02.680 --> 00:02:03.500
her powers.

00:02:03.500 --> 00:02:07.711
At the end of the episode "Valhalla," she
fights Five in a factory, and the melee [MEY-ley]

00:02:07.720 --> 00:02:10.940
ends with Five's foot on her throat and with
the Handler revealing herself.

00:02:10.940 --> 00:02:15.600
However, three times during their fight, Five
expects Lila to be one place, and she appears

00:02:15.600 --> 00:02:16.300
in another.

00:02:16.300 --> 00:02:20.080
Initially, we're supposed to assume she's
just unbelievably sneaky and fast.

00:02:20.080 --> 00:02:24.140
But when you rewatch the scene with the knowledge
of Lila's superpower, it becomes clear she's

00:02:24.140 --> 00:02:25.940
mirroring Five's teleportation.

00:02:25.940 --> 00:02:30.400
The camera isn't on her when she uses it,
so we never see the ripple effect, thus keeping

00:02:30.400 --> 00:02:32.700
her power a secret until the big showdown.

00:02:33.160 --> 00:02:36.920
In "The Swedish Job," we get a quick look
at an unnamed character who's a pretty clear

00:02:36.920 --> 00:02:40.720
reference to one of the most acclaimed graphic
novels of all time Watchmen.

00:02:40.720 --> 00:02:44.819
Just after Luther starts off on a morning
jog outside his rooming house, an elderly

00:02:44.820 --> 00:02:49.040
man carrying a placard reading "The END is
NIGH" appears and starts walking in the opposite

00:02:49.040 --> 00:02:49.940
direction.

00:02:49.940 --> 00:02:51.400
So what's the reference here?

00:02:51.400 --> 00:02:55.349
Well, carrying a placard that warns of the
end of the world is just about all the vigilante

00:02:55.349 --> 00:02:58.540
Rorschach seems
to do in Watchmen when he's out of costume.

00:02:58.540 --> 00:03:02.200
And while the man in "The Swedish Job" doesn't
look at all like Rorschach, with his long

00:03:02.200 --> 00:03:06.860
hair and full beard, he does have a passing
resemblance to Alan Moore, the author of Watchmen.

00:03:06.860 --> 00:03:09.380
And it's a fitting reference for a couple
of reasons.

00:03:09.380 --> 00:03:14.010
For one, just as the escalating tensions between
the United States and the Soviet Union provide

00:03:14.010 --> 00:03:18.130
Ozymandias' motivations in Watchmen, it's
an apocalyptic conflict between these two

00:03:18.130 --> 00:03:21.580
superpowers that Five is struggling to avoid
in The Umbrella Academy.

00:03:21.580 --> 00:03:26.580
For another, just like Watchmen's Ozymandias,
Five employs some pretty reprehensible tactics

00:03:26.580 --> 00:03:31.510
in saving the world, like traveling to 1982
and assassinating most of the Commission's

00:03:31.510 --> 00:03:36.360
executive board with an ax, paving the way
for the Handler's takeover of the organization.

00:03:36.600 --> 00:03:38.200
"She sent you, didn't she?"

00:03:38.260 --> 00:03:39.780
"Does it really matter now?"

00:03:40.180 --> 00:03:44.900
In the show's first season, we met Pogo, a
sophisticated chimpanzee butler with human

00:03:44.900 --> 00:03:47.220
intelligence who is sadly killed by Vanya.

00:03:47.220 --> 00:03:51.280
If you've ever wondered about the ape's origin
story, the second season has got your answers.

00:03:51.280 --> 00:03:55.470
"Valhalla" opens with a flashback of the younger
Pogo as a part of an early project to see

00:03:55.470 --> 00:03:59.640
how chimpanzees would be affected by space
flight and, by extension, what might be in

00:03:59.640 --> 00:04:01.240
store for human pilots.

00:04:01.240 --> 00:04:05.400
And two episodes later in "Öga for Öga,"
there's an easy-to-miss detail that could

00:04:05.400 --> 00:04:08.360
point to Pogo being much more famous than
we realized.

00:04:08.360 --> 00:04:13.299
When Five travels to 1982 Wisconsin to kill
the Commission's executive board, he stops

00:04:13.300 --> 00:04:15.220
at a vending machine for a candy bar.

00:04:15.220 --> 00:04:19.120
The one he winds up with is called a Fudge
Nutter, but right next to that bar is one

00:04:19.120 --> 00:04:20.480
called Pogo Gogos.

00:04:20.480 --> 00:04:23.620
And above the name on the wrapper, it reads,
"Take flight with..."

00:04:23.620 --> 00:04:25.800
It's a small detail, but it says a lot.

00:04:25.800 --> 00:04:30.140
For Pogo to have a candy bar named after him
suggests the intelligent chimp was much more

00:04:30.140 --> 00:04:32.380
of a Space Age icon than we realized.

00:04:32.960 --> 00:04:36.800
In the season premiere, while all the members
of the Umbrella Academy arrive in the same

00:04:36.800 --> 00:04:38.640
place, they don't arrive in the same year.

00:04:38.980 --> 00:04:45.000
"They're gone, like a fart in the wind.

00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:46.180
Poof."

00:04:46.440 --> 00:04:50.600
They fall from the portal into the same Dallas
alley, one at a time or two, in the case of

00:04:50.600 --> 00:04:54.540
Klaus and the ghost of Ben between 1960 and
1963.

00:04:54.540 --> 00:04:58.500
Interestingly, right across the street from
the alley is a movie theater, and in at least

00:04:58.500 --> 00:05:02.780
three cases, the theater marquee is advertising
a very specific kind of movie.

00:05:02.780 --> 00:05:08.039
When Klaus and the ghost of Ben appear in
1960, the marquee is advertising the 1959

00:05:08.040 --> 00:05:09.380
film Curse of the Undead.

00:05:09.380 --> 00:05:14.300
As Allison arrives in 1961, the theater has
apparently caught up with its releases, and

00:05:14.300 --> 00:05:17.060
the marquee announces that year's The Curse
of the Werewolf.

00:05:17.060 --> 00:05:21.880
The marquee during Luther's arrival in 1962
isn't really visible enough to read, but the

00:05:21.890 --> 00:05:27.750
same film is playing during Diego's appearance
in 1963, when Vanya shows up a month after

00:05:27.750 --> 00:05:32.040
Diego, and when Five arrives in November 1963
The Kiss of the Vampire.

00:05:32.040 --> 00:05:36.600
Of course, in Five's case, the fighting between
the US and Soviet forces has blown most of

00:05:36.600 --> 00:05:40.200
the letters off the marquee, and we doubt
the theater is still selling tickets at that

00:05:40.200 --> 00:05:40.700
point.

00:05:40.800 --> 00:05:44.680
It may be that the marquees are meant to reflect
the heroes' collective doubt expressed throughout

00:05:44.680 --> 00:05:49.680
the season about whether or not they're heroes,
superpowered freaks, or as the marquees reflect

00:05:49.680 --> 00:05:50.380
monsters.

00:05:50.380 --> 00:05:53.639
In other words, it's not exactly the kind
of thing you want to see when you're suddenly

00:05:53.640 --> 00:05:56.700
lost in the past and struggling with self-esteem
issues.

00:05:57.360 --> 00:06:01.780
In the Umbrella Academy comics, the heroes'
hometown is left intentionally ambiguous.

00:06:01.780 --> 00:06:04.580
In fact, it's referred to only as "The City."

00:06:04.580 --> 00:06:08.979
But it looks like season 2 of the series has
revealed the location of the Academy's HQ,

00:06:08.980 --> 00:06:11.420
at least as far as the TV adaptation is concerned.

00:06:11.420 --> 00:06:15.680
In a flashback in "Valhalla," we learn that
as soon as he could, Luther gets on a Greyhound

00:06:15.680 --> 00:06:17.760
bus in the hopes that he can find his father.

00:06:17.760 --> 00:06:21.980
During the trip, there's an overlay revealing
the bus' progress on a map, showing that Luther

00:06:21.980 --> 00:06:24.840
gets off somewhere in or around Indianapolis.

00:06:24.840 --> 00:06:27.440
It's an interesting choice for a superhero
HQ.

00:06:27.440 --> 00:06:31.760
Maybe the writers simply didn't want yet one
more superhero team hailing from the Big Apple.

00:06:31.760 --> 00:06:35.740
Maybe it just seemed far enough away from
Dallas to be a long trip but not too long.

00:06:35.740 --> 00:06:40.580
Or maybe Reginald Hargreeves is a big basketball
fan and just wanted to be near the Hoosiers.

00:06:40.960 --> 00:06:43.960
When it comes to powers, Diego has a pretty
unique skill set.

00:06:43.960 --> 00:06:47.979
The dude is a master of knives, able to make
blades spin through the air with the greatest

00:06:47.980 --> 00:06:50.380
of ease and fly in any direction he chooses.

00:06:50.380 --> 00:06:54.560
In the season finale, however, Diego uses
his powers in a way that we've never seen

00:06:54.560 --> 00:06:55.080
before.

00:06:55.080 --> 00:06:59.000
When the Commission's goons are firing on
him and Five, Diego buys Five the time he

00:06:59.009 --> 00:07:03.039
needs to make it to the house by jumping out
from behind cover and stopping the hail of

00:07:03.040 --> 00:07:05.560
bullets mid-air, a la Neo from The Matrix.

00:07:05.560 --> 00:07:09.820
Speaking to Den of Geek, showrunner Steven
Blackman said that this is an extension of

00:07:09.820 --> 00:07:12.380
the powers we've already seen before, explaining:

00:07:12.380 --> 00:07:17.220
"If you think about Diego's power, he can,
with his mind, control the trajectory of objects.

00:07:17.220 --> 00:07:21.320
Basically, he likes throwing, so he can throw
his knife, and he can make it go in weird,

00:07:21.320 --> 00:07:22.880
odd curvatures and directions."

00:07:22.880 --> 00:07:26.910
Blackman went on to say that the aftermath
of Ben's death hurt the chances that most

00:07:26.910 --> 00:07:31.150
of the members of the Umbrella Academy would
reach their powers' full potential early on,

00:07:31.150 --> 00:07:34.680
and that fans should expect for the heroes
to keep learning more about their abilities.

00:07:34.800 --> 00:07:37.020
"Everybody wants to see powers all of a sudden."

00:07:37.360 --> 00:07:40.140
Looking back on season 2, we've already seen
some of that.

00:07:40.140 --> 00:07:44.720
For example, before season 2, Klaus has no
idea it's possible for a ghost to possess

00:07:44.730 --> 00:07:48.949
him, and when spectral cowboys save him from
falling in the season finale, he seems as

00:07:48.949 --> 00:07:50.540
surprised by it as anyone else.

00:07:50.540 --> 00:07:54.940
Likewise, even before she lost her memory,
Vanya had no idea she could use her powers

00:07:54.940 --> 00:07:59.009
to resuscitate someone — as she does with
Harlan after he drowns — or that doing so

00:07:59.009 --> 00:08:00.600
would transfer her powers to him.

00:08:00.600 --> 00:08:04.780
With every episode, the Umbrella Academy grows
stronger and stronger, and we can't wait to

00:08:04.780 --> 00:08:07.080
see what weird directions their powers will
go.

00:08:07.460 --> 00:08:11.780
One of the potential differences between the
Umbrella Academy show and the comics is that

00:08:11.780 --> 00:08:15.720
in the source material, Diego has the ability
to hold his breath indefinitely.

00:08:15.720 --> 00:08:19.919
So far, we haven't seen this power expressed
in the TV series... or have we?

00:08:19.920 --> 00:08:24.600
A Reddit user going by the name high_arcanist
noticed something strange in a funny scene.

00:08:24.600 --> 00:08:28.660
In "A Light Supper," the Academy members all
cram into an elevator on their way to meet

00:08:28.660 --> 00:08:29.480
with their father.

00:08:29.480 --> 00:08:34.240
Nobody is exactly looking forward to the meeting,
and at least one of the heroes is especially

00:08:34.240 --> 00:08:34.780
anxious.

00:08:34.780 --> 00:08:39.539
During the trip up, almost everybody on the
elevator reacts in surprise and disgust as

00:08:39.540 --> 00:08:42.640
we soon learn Luther experiences some unpleasant
flatulence.

00:08:42.760 --> 00:08:46.460
But there's one person who doesn't react as
negatively as the rest Diego.

00:08:46.460 --> 00:08:50.920
While everyone else is scowling and covering
their mouths and noses, Diego is smiling,

00:08:50.920 --> 00:08:52.180
seeming amused at the whole thing.

00:08:52.180 --> 00:08:56.240
At no point does he seem to be suffering from
the stench as his brothers and sisters are.

00:08:56.240 --> 00:08:59.860
This, high_arcanist believes, is because the
Diego of the show shares the breath-holding

00:08:59.860 --> 00:09:03.930
ability of his comic book counterpart, and
so he's able to avoid most of the negative

00:09:03.930 --> 00:09:08.140
aspects of the experience and enjoy the discomfort
of his adoptive siblings.

00:09:08.140 --> 00:09:12.540
It certainly seems possible, though it could
also be that Diego just has a stronger stomach

00:09:12.540 --> 00:09:14.100
for his brother's "nervousness."

00:09:14.620 --> 00:09:18.840
In "The Seven Stages," Five enlists Luther's
help to do something that he warns is very

00:09:18.850 --> 00:09:21.810
dangerous to contact an earlier version of
himself.

00:09:21.810 --> 00:09:27.080
Doing this, Five warns, is incredibly risky
because of what he calls Paradox Psychosis,

00:09:27.080 --> 00:09:30.840
which is a kind of psychosis a person may
undergo when encountering themselves in another

00:09:30.840 --> 00:09:31.460
time.

00:09:31.460 --> 00:09:36.140
There are seven stages of paradox psychosis,
and we watch both Five and Old Man Five go

00:09:36.140 --> 00:09:37.400
through all those stages.

00:09:37.780 --> 00:09:39.460
"You just itched your neck.

00:09:39.460 --> 00:09:41.460
That's stage two of paradox psychosis."

00:09:41.460 --> 00:09:42.240
"No I didn't.

00:09:42.240 --> 00:09:43.240
I didn't itch my neck."

00:09:43.240 --> 00:09:44.240
"Denial is stage one."

00:09:44.640 --> 00:09:47.860
It's possible we see someone else go through
them earlier in the season.

00:09:47.870 --> 00:09:52.399
Two of the seven stages are excessive gas
and homicidal rage, and in "A Light Supper,"

00:09:52.400 --> 00:09:56.240
we see Luther exhibit both, on the elevator
and at dinner with their father.

00:09:58.600 --> 00:10:00.400
"Look at what you did to me!

00:10:01.240 --> 00:10:02.840
Look at it!"

00:10:03.180 --> 00:10:07.460
And that kind of anger especially directed
at his dad is pretty unusual for our hulking

00:10:07.460 --> 00:10:08.080
hero.

00:10:08.080 --> 00:10:11.680
Blackman has said to expect things in season
2 to hint toward the future.

00:10:11.680 --> 00:10:15.300
Could it be that another Luther maybe one
from a future season is lurking somewhere

00:10:15.300 --> 00:10:16.220
nearby?

00:10:16.220 --> 00:10:19.980
Of course, that would lead to the question
of why he isn't exhibiting the other symptoms.

00:10:19.980 --> 00:10:24.140
Although it could be that wherever the future
Luther is hiding, perhaps he isn't as close

00:10:24.140 --> 00:10:28.420
to the 1963 Luther as the Fives were to each
other or for quite as long.

